> 🔍 **TL;DR** — The rupee's long-run value over 2013–2025 is driven almost entirely by **net FPI (portfolio) flows** and **global risk aversion (VIX)** — not by the classical interest-rate-parity channel. Interest differentials and the forward premium matter a lot in the *short run* but vanish as long-run anchors: a textbook restatement of the **forward-premium puzzle**.

---

## 📖 Overview

This repository contains a single, self-contained research notebook (`Model-2 (ARDL).ipynb`) that investigates what drives the **INR/USD exchange rate** between **January 2013 and December 2025** — a window spanning the 2013 taper tantrum, the COVID-19 shock, and the 2022 Fed hiking cycle.

Because the underlying macro-financial series exhibit a **mixed order of integration** (some I(0), some I(1)), the study is built around the **ARDL bounds-testing approach** (Pesaran, Shin & Smith, 2001) rather than a standard Johansen/VECM framework, with a **partitioned VECM** run alongside it as a robustness cross-check.

## 🧭 Notebook Structure

| Part | Section | What it does |
|:---:|---|---|
| 1 | ⚙️ Environment Setup & Library Imports | Loads `pandas`, `numpy`, `statsmodels`, `arch`, plotting config |
| 2 | 📥 Data Import & Initial Inspection | Loads and profiles the raw monthly macro-financial dataset |
| 3 | 🧩 Missing Value Imputation | Linear interpolation & forward-fill for isolated gaps |
| 4 | 🔁 Data Manipulation | Converts INR-denominated series (M3, GDP) to USD Million |
| 5 | 🛠️ Feature Engineering | Builds CAB/GDP, interest-rate differentials, real rates, RBI intervention proxy, FPI totals, log transforms |
| 6 | 📊 Exploratory Data Analysis | Correlation structure, rolling correlations, structural-break visuals |
| 7 | 🧪 Unit Root Tests | ADF + KPSS + Phillips-Perron, with a trend-inclusive pass and a no-trend robustness pass |
| 8 | 📐 ARDL Bounds Test | Estimates the UECM, tests for a long-run cointegrating relationship |
| 9 | 🔄 Partitioned VECM | Johansen cointegration on the I(1) subsystem as a robustness check |
| 10 | 🧠 Interpretation | Economic reading of long-run vs. short-run coefficients |
| 11 | ✅ Conclusion | Synthesis, policy implications, and limitations |

## 🧮 Key Variables

| Variable | Description | Theoretical channel |
|---|---|---|
| `LOG_INR_USD` | Log nominal exchange rate | Dependent variable |
| `NOM_INT_DIFF_MKT` | India 91-day T-Bill − US 3-month T-Bill yield | Uncovered Interest Parity (UIP) |
| `3M_ForwardPremium_IND(%)` | 3-month forward premium | Covered Interest Parity |
| `FPI_Total` | Net FPI equity + debt flows | Portfolio-balance channel |
| `LOG_VIX_US` | Log US VIX | Global financial cycle / risk aversion |
| `RBI_INTV` | Δ RBI Foreign Currency Assets | Central bank intervention |
| `LOG_OIL` | Log Brent crude price | Commodity / import-cost channel |
| `CAB_PCT_GDP` | Current Account Balance as % of GDP | External sustainability |

## 🔬 Methodology at a Glance

- **Why ARDL, not just Johansen?** ARDL bounds testing (Pesaran, Shin & Smith, 2001) is valid for **any mix of I(0)/I(1) regressors**, has better small-sample properties, avoids the degrees-of-freedom cost of VAR systems, and delivers short-run *and* long-run coefficients from a single equation.
- **Cross-checked stationarity:** ADF, KPSS, and Phillips-Perron are run in tandem — and re-run **without a deterministic trend** — because a trend-inclusive specification was found to spuriously classify `LOG_INR_USD` as stationary, which would invalidate the entire cointegration analysis.
- **Robustness:** A **partitioned VECM** restricted to the I(1) variables (with I(0) regressors entered as exogenous controls) tests whether the ARDL conclusion survives under an entirely different estimator.

## 📈 Headline Findings

- ✅ **Strong evidence of a long-run equilibrium relationship** — the ARDL bounds F-statistic (5.93) rejects the no-cointegration null beyond the 99.9% upper bound.
- 🌍 **Global risk (VIX)** and **net FPI flows** are the *only* long-run drivers that are both statistically significant and correctly signed.
- 📉 **Classical UIP variables fail in the long run:** the interest-rate differential and forward premium are insignificant at the long-run horizon, despite being highly significant short-run drivers — a modern restatement of the **forward-premium puzzle** (Fama, 1984).
- 🐢 **The rupee is weakly exogenous:** it does not self-correct quickly toward equilibrium (~3.5% monthly adjustment, implying an ~19-month half-life); instead the forward premium and oil price do most of the adjusting.
- ⚖️ **One honest point of tension:** the Johansen trace test on the restricted I(1) subsystem falls just short of the 5% critical value, whereas ARDL rejects decisively — reconciled by the fact that ARDL's explanatory power is concentrated in two *stationary* variables (FPI, VIX) excluded from the Johansen system by construction.

## 🧰 Tech Stack

- **Language:** Python 3.13
- **Core libraries:** `pandas`, `numpy`, `matplotlib`, `seaborn`
- **Econometrics:** `statsmodels` (`tsa.stattools`, `tsa.ardl.UECM`, `tsa.vector_ar`), `arch.unitroot.PhillipsPerron`, `scipy.stats`

## 🚀 Getting Started

```bash
# Clone the repository
git clone <your-repo-url>
cd <your-repo-name>

# Install dependencies
pip install pandas numpy matplotlib seaborn statsmodels arch scipy jupyter

# Launch the notebook
jupyter notebook "Model-2 (ARDL).ipynb"
```

## ⚠️ Limitations

- Small sample (n ≈ 155 monthly observations) limits test power, especially for Johansen's trace test.
- The I(0)/I(1) classification of `LOG_INR_USD` is sensitive to the deterministic-trend specification and required a theory-driven judgment call.
- Rolling correlations suggest the true relationship may be **regime-dependent** (e.g., around COVID and the 2022 hiking cycle) — a linear ARDL/VECM estimates a single average relationship and cannot capture this.
- Natural extensions: nonlinear/threshold ARDL (Shin, Yu & Greenwood-Nimmo, 2014) and formal structural-break testing (Bai-Perron).

## 📚 Key References

- Pesaran, M. H., Shin, Y., & Smith, R. J. (2001). *Bounds testing approaches to the analysis of level relationships.*
- Fama, E. F. (1984). *Forward and spot exchange rates.*
- Gabaix, X., & Maggiori, M. (2015). *International liquidity and exchange rate dynamics.*
- Gourinchas, P.-O., & Rey, H. (2005). *International financial adjustment.*
- Meese, R. A., & Rogoff, K. (1983). *Empirical exchange rate models of the seventies.*

